Pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order kinetic models are commonly used to describe the adsorption rate. The equations for the pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order kinetic models are given as follows, respectively54:3\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\:{q}_{t}=\:{q}_{e}\left(1-{e}^{{k}_{1}t}\right)$$\end{document}4\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\:{q}_{t}=\frac{{q}_{e}^{2}{k}_{2}t}{{1+{q}_{e}k}_{2}t}$$\end{document}where qe and qt (mg/g) represent the amounts of strontium adsorbed at equilibrium and at time t (min), respectively, and k1 (1/min) and k2 (g/mg·min) are the equilibrium rate constants for the pseudo-first-order and pseudo-second-order adsorption models, respectively. The results of kinetic modeling of this process using pseudo first and second order models are presented in Fig. 8. The obtained constants of kinetic models are presented in Table 1. According to the results (R2 and RMSE values), the empirical kinetic data are in better agreement with the Pseudo-second-order kinetic model for all studied adsorbents, which implies some type of collision or interaction between pairs of independent sites of adsorption during the rate-limiting step55.

The Langmuir isotherm model is expressed by Eq. (5) 56.5\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\:{q}_{e}=\frac{{q}_{m}b{C}_{e}}{1+b{C}_{e}}$$\end{document}where qe is the amount of metal adsorbed per unit mass of adsorbent (mg/g), Ce is the equilibrium solution concentration (mg/L), b is the Langmuir constant (L/mg), and qm is the maximum adsorption capacity (mg/g).

The Freundlich isotherm model is expressed by Eq. (6) 56.6\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$\:{q}_{e}={k}_{f}{{C}_{e}}^{\frac{1}{n}}$$\end{document}where kf (L/g) is the Freundlich constant related to adsorption capacity, and n is the dimensionless Freundlich constant associated with bond strength. The isotherm modeling results are shown in Fig. 9. The values of the isotherm modeling parameters were shown in Table 2. According to the results (R2 and RMSE values), the Langmuir model fits the experimental data better for all adsorbents studied. This suggests that the adsorption mechanism involves monolayer coverage and that the adsorbent surface is homogeneous with respect to adsorption energy. As shown in Table 2, the Langmuir model predicts the maximum adsorption rate of 42.02, 60.98, and 65.79 mg/g adsorbent for Amberlite XAD-7, Amberlite-TOPO SIRs with impregnation ratios of 0.386 and 0.473 g/g SIR, respectively.
